## What is inside

| Crate | Path | |
|---|---|---|
| `srt-sys` | `src/srt-sys` | Hand-written Windows FFI: kernel32, user32, DXGI and D3D12 COM vtables, TEB/PEB. |
| `srt-loader` | `src/srt-loader` | PE32+ parser, mapper and import resolver (relocations, TLS, `.pdata`, delay imports, reports). |
| `srt-shader` | `src/srt-shader` | RDNA2 decoder, IR and a direct DXIL emitter (own LLVM bitcode writer, container and hash), with a CPU interpreter and a GPU test runner. |
| `srt-gfx` | `src/srt-gfx` | The console Direct3D 12 on PC Direct3D 12: unified memory, descriptors, root arguments, command records, GFX10 tiling, presentation and pacing, pipeline cache. |
| `srt-texcodec` | `src/srt-texcodec` | Decoder for the console's hardware texture codec into standard BCn blocks. |
| `srt` | `src/srt` | `srt.exe`: the platform layer (kernelx, xmem, XGameRuntime, XAudio2 and the audio processor, GameInput, DirectStorage, file system overlay), the CLI, and the mod and plugin host. |
| `srt-bench` | `src/srt-bench` | A small benchmark harness (wall time, CPU time, peak memory). |
| `srt-tests` | `tests` | Integration tests, including a console-style test title built with MSVC. |

`probes/` holds two stand-alone measurement programs (audio processor, file throughput) and
`tools/` a few developer scripts. Documentation is in [`docs/`](docs/).

## Design

- **From scratch.** Written without reference to other implementations' source, from public
  documentation (Microsoft Learn, the PE/COFF specification, AMD's published RDNA2 ISA and
  addrlib data, the DXIL and LLVM bitcode documentation) and from observing behaviour.
- **Standard library only.** No crates.io dependencies. Windows APIs are declared by hand.
  The only third-party material is a set of constant model tables used by the texture codec;
  their origins and licences are listed in
  [`src/srt-texcodec/NOTICE.md`](src/srt-texcodec/NOTICE.md).
- **No shader compiler.** Console GPU machine code is translated straight to DXIL; there is no
  HLSL step and no DXC or FXC at run time.
- **Software page table for GPU memory.** Title memory is exposed to the GPU as heaps, and
  translated programs reach console addresses through a page table, so the console's unified
  memory model works without copying.

More in [`docs/architecture.md`](docs/architecture.md) and
[`docs/design-principles.md`](docs/design-principles.md).

## Build and run

Requirements: Windows 10 or 11 (x64), a Direct3D 12 capable GPU (WARP works for tests), Rust
stable (`x86_64-pc-windows-msvc`) and the MSVC build tools (for the linker and the test title).

```
cargo build --release
cargo test --release -- --test-threads=4
```

Known issue: the GPU ray-tracing test (`cargo test -p srt --test raytracing`) has crashed
intermittently when the machine was under heavy load from other GPU work. It passes when run on
its own. The cause is not yet known.

To run a title you own, point `srt` at the folder that contains it (the folder with
`MicrosoftGame.config` or `appxmanifest.xml` and the executable):

```
target\release\srt.exe info  "<title folder>"            # import resolution report
target\release\srt.exe run   "<title folder>" --data "<data folder>"
```

Everything a run writes (save data, the title's own file writes, logs, `report.json`) goes to the
data folder, by default `%LOCALAPPDATA%\SonomaRuntime\titles\<identity>`. An optional
per-title profile can be placed there ([`docs/title-profile.md`](docs/title-profile.md)).
`tools/run-example.ps1` is a small launcher with a time limit. See
[`docs/building.md`](docs/building.md) and [`docs/runtime-contract.md`](docs/runtime-contract.md).

## What it does not do

- It does not remove, bypass or fake copy protection, licence or entitlement checks, or
  integrity checks. The store API is not implemented: a title asking for it gets "class not
  registered", and nothing is forwarded to Gaming Services.
- It does not connect to Xbox or any other online service.
- It does not include, download or decrypt any game. You must supply a title you own.

## Safety

- **Network gate.** WinHTTP, socket and GDK HTTP calls made by title code go through a gate.
  Xbox service hosts are always refused. Other hosts are refused too unless
  `SRT_ALLOW_NETWORK=1` is set. Loopback stays open for local IPC.
- **Read-only title folder.** The title folder is never written to; the title's writes go to an
  overlay in the data folder.
- **Software adapter first.** The GPU tests run on the WARP software adapter by default, and
  shader translations are checked against the CPU interpreter on WARP
  (`srt-shader-test --adapter warp`) before they are tried on hardware. Pipeline creation can be
  moved into worker processes with a memory cap and a per-pipeline time limit.
- The mod menu's trainer never writes values that its data marks as protected or read-only.

